00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.440
Strongly agree, strongly disagree.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.800
Answer in the comments below

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.487
and suggest a prompt on our Kickstarter.

00:00:11.487 --> 00:00:13.606
- I thoroughly enjoy fried chicken.

00:00:13.606 --> 00:00:15.860
I understand the stereotypes about it

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.150
and all that kinda stuff.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.710
- That chicken thing, man.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:19.780
- The chicken.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:20.770
- Chicken is just not...

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.480
That's just not something
that black people enjoy.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.243
You name me one group of people
that don't like a Yardbird.

00:00:27.243 --> 00:00:30.410
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:40.482
- [Interrogator] Kanye West
is invited to the cookout.

00:00:40.482 --> 00:00:41.315
(all laughing)

00:00:41.315 --> 00:00:44.619
- No he ain't doing that.
He's not doing that.

00:00:44.619 --> 00:00:46.603
I know you didn't do that.

00:00:46.603 --> 00:00:48.537
(laughter drowns voices)

00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:49.518
- I told you I was leaving you this time.

00:00:49.518 --> 00:00:50.960
- Then you're talking about change.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.399
They're about to go worse.
Let's just be honest.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:55.800
- Well, last time I was, agree

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.690
because I was like, he's
just the crazy junk uncle,

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.290
but now he's a little too disruptive.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.829
I don't want him at my cookout.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.500
- He's got a lot of
things he's going through.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.500
So I'm not expecting him to
change anything right now

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.000
but right now he's just still.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.570
He needs some time, figure
out life, then come eat.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.860
- I'm doubling down,
again he's misunderstood.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.630
People like Kanye are creatives

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.620
and he uses like creative speech.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.340
- Like I don't think that he's so creative

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.190
and that his words are
being misinterpreted,

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.380
he's making very specific phrases,

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.010
like black people chose to be slaves.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.150
That's completely untrue

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.100
and in my opinion,
that's just unacceptable.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.910
- But I think that's
people misunderstanding

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.010
what he's saying.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.360
And let's be real, nobody including Kanye,

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.430
doesn't think that people got in line

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.210
to get shackled to be a slave.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.680
This is part of the
problem the media cut off,

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.660
kind of like the overall meaning
of what he's trying to say.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.920
People were angry that he was Republican

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.610
or that he was voting for Trump.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.750
What he was basically
saying is you're not...

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.640
Don't be a slave to like
the Democratic Party.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.780
I think there's an identity politics issue

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.950
in the black community.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.930
You're not allowed to think freely

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.840
and he is to me, another
member of my community

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.510
and I want him on my cookout--

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.870
- Invite him over your
house then for a barbecue.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.020
- Kanye West, you're invited.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:11.149
- Would you let Kanye teach your children?

00:02:11.149 --> 00:02:13.170
- I think Kanye West,
- That's a yes or no.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.961
- Is not a bad person.

00:02:14.961 --> 00:02:16.830
- No. I'm not saying his bad person.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:17.663
- I'm not saying he's a bad person either.

00:02:17.663 --> 00:02:19.920
- That has nothing to do
about him as a person.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.440
- But I wouldn't let the grand wizard

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.408
give my children a history list.

00:02:23.408 --> 00:02:24.958
- [Interrogator] Dave Chappelle

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.940
- I have loved ones and friends and family

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.450
who are like part of the queer community

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.620
and I have friends who are trans

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.054
and talking to them about
like the last couple specials

00:02:41.054 --> 00:02:44.680
and just sort of like
his comments in general

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.610
about trans people.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:47.970
It has kind of rubbed me the wrong way,

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.560
which is like it sucks

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.110
cause I really did love Chappelle show.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.380
I loved a lot of his standup specials

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.000
but like over the years it's just like,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.720
okay, well now I'm seeing
it through a different lines

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.810
because other people are
bringing things too light

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.870
that I probably wasn't thinking
about when I watched it.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.920
- I'm a big Dave Chappelle. I love comedy.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.843
He was trying to navigate a
very, very narrow channel.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.540
I think he did it quite
well, cause he's a comedian,

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.600
they're there to push
the boundaries are funny.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.240
And at the same time what you were saying

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.073
you gotta be careful.

00:03:22.073 --> 00:03:23.430
So I agree with you on that.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.410
But after hearing Chappelle explain,

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.860
sort of when he was on stage
after he did The Closer.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.117
- He came back.

00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:30.950
- After hearing,

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.040
I don't think he had
any malice in his heart.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.030
- I think we need to learn how
to not apologize for people

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.760
because of their historical importance.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.570
Just because I love the Chappelle show

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.570
doesn't mean I'm gonna
stand up for The Closer.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:44.800
It's not always just a joke

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.260
cause it might be just a joke to you,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.492
it's not just a joke to that
person who just got murdered.

00:03:48.492 --> 00:03:49.942
(logo whooshing)

00:03:49.942 --> 00:03:52.750
- [Interrogator] Black
people are more homophobic

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.343
than other races.

00:03:57.852 --> 00:04:00.172
- So I almost say this I'm an avid golfer.

00:04:00.172 --> 00:04:04.060
So a lot of my friends who
I play golf with are white

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.420
and they say some stuff
too when it comes to this.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.253
So that's why I was like,

00:04:07.253 --> 00:04:09.070
I gotta come with somewhat disagree,

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.990
but, I only can speak for our community.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:15.413
We are a little rough
on the LGBTQ community.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:19.190
- Why do you guys think
this prompt is asked?

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.120
- Cause there's a lot of rappers

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.140
who say homophobic stuff all the time.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.557
- Yeah, and the black church.

00:04:24.557 --> 00:04:27.920
I think religion plays
a big part of homophobia

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.780
and like our society today

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:31.531
but it's not just the black church.

00:04:31.531 --> 00:04:34.070
- [Interrogator] America
would be better off

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.023
if only black people voted.

00:04:38.524 --> 00:04:40.760
- I think our votes
would pretty much reflect

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:45.760
what would be good for
the outcome of America.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.950
- Even for the LGBTQ community?

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.420
- Yeah. I believe so. I believe so.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.423
- I'm not saying you wrong.

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:52.340
I'm just asking things

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.410
that are just within our own community

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.350
that we don't all agree on.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.183
- That's a good question.

00:04:56.183 --> 00:04:57.750
I think the majority of us

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.872
will actually be for equal rights.

00:04:59.872 --> 00:05:01.420
Black people know what it's like

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.780
to be discriminated against.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.060
- I think anytime you
have a homogenous group

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.530
that's making decisions

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.210
you're inevitably gonna have problems.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:10.890
So, I think it might start off

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.860
with this idealist way of like,

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:13.900
yes, let's vote for things

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:15.260
that are gonna make everybody happy

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.750
but eventually like power
just corrupts people.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.270
So if we're the only ones voting

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.950
we're gonna start to do
stuff that advantages us

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.292
especially given our history here.

00:05:22.292 --> 00:05:25.303
- [Interrogator] Black
people can be racist.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.270
- I think somebody told
me about how racism exists

00:05:36.251 --> 00:05:37.810
within the space of power.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.630
And you have to look at it in terms of

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.760
racism comes from having
the power for your ideals

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.110
to actually cause harm to other people

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.420
or disadvantage other people

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:49.130
and so most of the time,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.380
a racist black person
doesn't have enough power

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.506
to harm somebody that
they're racist against.

00:05:53.506 --> 00:05:54.557
- Are you saying there aren't black people

00:05:54.557 --> 00:05:56.363
who can hire and fire people?

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.510
- No.(murmuring in the background)

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.500
- Well I hire and fire people all type.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:01.540
- What's power to you then?

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.247
- Racism is a specific type of prejudice.

00:06:04.247 --> 00:06:05.920
Everyone could be prejudice.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.010
- Can a Chinese person be racist?

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.000
- In China? Yes.
- How about here?

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.280
- I think in America,

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.910
it's all predominantly white run society.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:17.660
So I think as a racial,
a precious system--

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.490
- So a Chinese person cannot be racist

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.280
to black people in the United States.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.009
- I think they could be
prejudice to black people.

00:06:23.009 --> 00:06:26.300
I think they can influence
individual black people.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.900
But lemme say this, I look
at racism as a system.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.350
- I think context matters
in this situation here.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:34.039
So can you look at it like this,

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:36.500
when you just break, can
a black person be racist?

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:37.420
Yes.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.833
Can a black person be racist in America?

00:06:39.833 --> 00:06:41.770
I don't really know. Think about it.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:44.250
How does a black person
being a racist in America

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.453
benefits a black person?

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:47.260
- That's my point.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:48.093
- Think about it.

00:06:48.093 --> 00:06:50.170
How if I wanna be racist in America

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:51.434
how can I get ahead...

00:06:51.434 --> 00:06:53.270
I'm gonna set out to be a black racist.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:55.690
- But getting ahead, isn't
the criteria to be racist

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.170
- But racism that's where it start

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.896
and bro it's like one of the main engines.

00:07:00.896 --> 00:07:02.993
- So we're gonna use different words.

00:07:02.993 --> 00:07:04.174
What you guys are basically saying is that

00:07:04.174 --> 00:07:07.000
in order to be racist,

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:08.460
you can only be white
in the United States.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:11.490
- I'm just saying the impact is bigger.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:12.940
So the judgment around what it is.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:17.356
None of it's right, but the
impact is widely different.

00:07:17.356 --> 00:07:20.090
- [Interrogator] The U.S.
should pay black people

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:21.423
reparations.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.290
The U.S. should pay
black people reparations.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.420
- You gonna wait.
(Christian and Efa laughing)

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.203
- [Interrogator] Three, two, one, go.

00:07:29.293 --> 00:07:30.626
- Without doubt.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.178
- I will say though, it's
not just black people.

00:07:35.178 --> 00:07:36.500
U.S. owes a lot of people money.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.720
- The U.S. doesn't exist
without black help,

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:41.605
African Americans.

00:07:41.605 --> 00:07:43.010
- (Christian murmuring) at every stage.

00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:44.190
- I'd like to correct it though.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.560
I think something should be done

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.951
for the descendants of slaves.

00:07:48.951 --> 00:07:50.637
- That's why I was like,
I wanna come speak more.

00:07:50.637 --> 00:07:51.927
- I took it as black Americans.

00:07:51.927 --> 00:07:54.150
I took it as black Americans, because--

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.850
- Cause I feel like my
family just got here

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:57.683
in the seventies.

00:07:57.683 --> 00:08:00.850
So do I feel like I deserve
what a person deserves

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:03.407
whose family has literally
purchased in slavery? No.

00:08:03.407 --> 00:08:05.350
- No. The question you
asked was a good question.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.540
Do you deserve, I think, yes.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:08.620
But how much do you deserve

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.893
in comparison to others
with longer history here?

00:08:11.893 --> 00:08:13.290
That's the next question?

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.040
- Why does a recent immigrant from Africa

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:16.435
that came maybe 10
years ago deserve money?

00:08:16.435 --> 00:08:17.439
- Because they have to deal with...

00:08:17.439 --> 00:08:20.060
There is systemic oppression
still happening today.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.610
So they're still impacted.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.030
It may not be the same degree,
but they're still impacted.

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:26.610
And reparation for me
doesn't mean here's $5

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:29.160
or here's a million dollars
one individual black person.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.070
It means what are we doing
to repair the community

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:36.310
that we have disenfranchised
in our society.

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:37.617
That's all it means to me.

00:08:37.617 --> 00:08:41.490
- Anyways, I think
reparations, the term to me

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.486
people look at it as like getting a check.

00:08:45.486 --> 00:08:48.090
- And a check need to be cut.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.440
Don't make no mistake about that.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.730
A check need to be cut just right.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:53.810
Because the wealth of this country

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.444
was stolen from our labor, bro.

00:08:58.444 --> 00:09:00.860
Don't think it's because you from Africa,

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:01.870
you don't deserve none of that either

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:05.020
because they did a number
on Africans as well.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:09.120
- [Interrogator] Police
target black men and women.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.723
Three, two, one, go.

00:09:12.909 --> 00:09:16.409
(high-pitched hiss sound)

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:27.640
- I like law and order, I like safety

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.770
and I do think for the most part,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:34.030
a lot of the police throughout the country

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:36.557
do it to serve their communities.

00:09:36.557 --> 00:09:39.630
But the last two years
has really showed me that

00:09:41.028 --> 00:09:45.350
we have a lot of problems and it's not...

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.550
It was emotional for me as well,

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:51.000
especially after the
George Floyd incident.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.920
But that wasn't even the
only one, there's so many.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.453
And it got to the point where,

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:59.870
I started to really ask myself is this,

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:02.480
how can you get law and order and safety

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:07.563
and still respect police,
but at the same time,

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:11.760
fight for change. It's hard.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.180
I don't know what to do.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:16.740
I don't think any of us
really have the solution.

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:19.550
- It was created to monitor
the minority in the group.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.450
Back in the day, it was
the Irish, it was Italians.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:25.500
And so it's ingrained into
the system of policing

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.450
and that's the problem.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:28.871
I think we have to reframe
what policing is now

00:10:28.871 --> 00:10:30.000
and until we do that,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.920
it will always be
targeting people of color

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:33.720
because that's what it was created to do.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.910
It's one of those things for me,

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:39.000
the phrasing of it is police
and that's a big term.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:39.833
That means,

00:10:39.833 --> 00:10:41.370
it's not an individual
person we're talking of.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:42.310
We're talking about an institution.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:45.410
We're talking about all the
people within that institution.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:47.630
The system, the institution is protecting,

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:50.480
the individuals who have
target people of color,

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:51.560
and we see them doing that

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.550
because the ones that get caught in media,

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:55.850
it's not the first infraction.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:57.350
So that means they were individuals

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:58.620
who were aware of what happened,

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:01.200
either being a partner
in the car with them

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.640
or on those streets with
them or a supervisor

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:04.970
or whoever it may be,

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.330
who allowed them to go back on the streets

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:09.300
in the same manner that they were before

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.880
and continue doing the same things.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:12.760
- And the institution makes money

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:15.170
from the targeting of black people.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.390
If the police don't arrest people,

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.440
prison can get no customers.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.040
- You gotta look at the prison
industrial complex here too

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.680
in the U.S, that's huge business.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:27.380
So, and if you look at a group of citizens

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.220
that have been perceived as no value

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.016
having to society or whatever,

00:11:32.016 --> 00:11:33.810
okay, boom, we're gonna push them.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.540
We're gonna pipeline
them into that system.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:37.780
- But also you have to remember

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:42.120
that like almost 20% of murderers is us.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.320
And like we're the
victims of a lot of crime

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:49.080
and so we are also the beneficiaries

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.760
of some of the protections that do happen.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.900
So it hurts us to have
like no type of protection

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:57.363
or safety as well.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:02.158
- We're not saying abolish
all forms of protection.

00:12:02.158 --> 00:12:03.600
We're saying we need to reframe

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.288
what that protection looks
like so that we can feel safe.

00:12:06.288 --> 00:12:08.290
Even though people say,

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.830
the statistics are saying
we're safer on the statistics

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.203
but really do you feel, does
the community feel safer?

00:12:12.203 --> 00:12:14.510
- [Interrogator] What
did you all think about

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:16.600
Derek Chauvin's guilty verdict?

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.930
- I feel like it's still
performative in some ways

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:19.763
like cool.

00:12:19.763 --> 00:12:21.240
All right, there's that verdict.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:22.580
Probably, there's gonna
be a video tomorrow

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:23.430
of somebody else.

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.840
So it doesn't feel like it
moves the needle very much

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:29.140
when it's just one very high profile case.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.120
Something needs to change for those people

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.010
who are supposed to be our protectors

00:12:33.010 --> 00:12:35.000
to actually protect us.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.050
I do wanna say that, you
said something about like

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:38.527
we're the ones who do the most
crime or something like that.

00:12:38.527 --> 00:12:40.710
- I said we are victims.

00:12:40.710 --> 00:12:41.674
- We're the most victims.

00:12:41.674 --> 00:12:43.930
And it's like, why are
we victims all the time?

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:46.700
Why are there being guns
put into our communities

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:47.533
at higher levels?

00:12:47.533 --> 00:12:50.980
Why are we getting sentenced
for certain offenses,

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:52.200
more heavily than other races?

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.600
- Cause the prison need customers.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.350
- Exactly and it's customers too.

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:57.365
It's not just (beep sound) prisoners.

00:12:57.365 --> 00:12:58.198
- We are the number one customer.

00:12:58.198 --> 00:13:00.180
- You're saying that we're victims

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.790
we're getting killed and robbed and hurt

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.060
we, we're getting hurt.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:05.050
- But why?

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:06.727
- Not because...

00:13:06.727 --> 00:13:08.500
I think that has nothing
to do with prison.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:09.459
- No, it does it.

00:13:09.459 --> 00:13:11.110
There's research that says

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.150
impoverished communities have more crime.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.330
It's not because they enjoy crime more,

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:16.710
it's because they're impoverished.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:19.010
It's because they need
money, they need to survive.

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:20.220
And so there's a cycle,

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:21.598
- They need economic (murmuring)

00:13:21.598 --> 00:13:25.430
- There's a cycle of prison,
which means less education

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:26.710
which means less income,

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:29.600
which means smaller money
coming to your community

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:32.020
which means less education,
which means more crime,

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:32.960
which means more prison.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.880
So the statistics about
our community making crime.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.399
Yes, but why is that cycle
happening in our community?

00:13:37.399 --> 00:13:40.300
- [Interrogator] I am proud to be black.

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.767
Three, two, one, go.

00:13:43.987 --> 00:13:45.400
- We all gonna go back to the same letter

00:13:47.376 --> 00:13:48.610
we went to last time.

00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:50.019
- Nothing's changed there.

00:13:50.019 --> 00:13:51.510
(Adib clapping)

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.905
- Glad to have you on the
same side as us for a while.

00:13:53.905 --> 00:13:55.210
- Finally.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.740
- I feel more proud of
our people every day.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:13:59.590
Cause it's another day we survived.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:02.290
Just even the slightest microaggressions

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:04.200
that happen to our community throughout

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.950
and yet we wake up and yet we get dressed

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:09.710
and yet we go out and
we do it again and again

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:13.120
because we have the hope of
a better future of change.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.440
I think that's something
to be very proud of

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.450
and to to never let slide,
always be proud of that.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.860
- I think the more I see
people gaining traction

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:25.088
like in America, changing to
where we're able to make moves

00:14:25.088 --> 00:14:27.850
in ways that we couldn't
before, the prouder I get.

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:30.570
So, it might sound silly to be excited

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.070
about seeing more black billionaires

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:34.410
or like a black, I think, miss America,

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:36.830
miss USA and miss something else.

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:38.826
All three were black women
for the first time ever.

00:14:38.826 --> 00:14:41.099
- I just wanna say for all
the people in the comments

00:14:41.099 --> 00:14:45.400
that were saying, I'm
not black, I'm black.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.640
Cause a lot of people were accusing me

00:14:49.714 --> 00:14:51.370
of basically not being black.

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:55.310
I think it's important to
remember that black is diverse

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:57.950
and that we have different types of hair,

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:01.607
different skin tones and anyways--

00:15:01.607 --> 00:15:02.448
- We're not a monolith.

00:15:02.448 --> 00:15:04.090
- We're not a monolith.

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:06.025
- We're not a monolith.

00:15:06.025 --> 00:15:09.192
(murmurs drown voice)

00:15:11.046 --> 00:15:13.122
- I actually spoke up
over you all this time.

00:15:13.122 --> 00:15:14.818
- You let him know.

00:15:14.818 --> 00:15:16.210
- I already know, black and black.

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:17.353
- Gonna let him know black and black.

00:15:17.353 --> 00:15:18.843
- Yes.

00:15:18.843 --> 00:15:19.843
- Thank you!

00:15:20.709 --> 00:15:23.405
(woman clapping in background)

00:15:23.405 --> 00:15:26.405
(gentle jazz music)

